---
import { STATIC_PAGE_PATHS } from "../../data/staticPages";

const firstStops = [
  {
    href: STATIC_PAGE_PATHS.aboutUs,
    title: "The Monastery",
    body:
      "Start here if you want to understand the living community in Shimla, its abbots, students, and educational life.",
  },
  {
    href: STATIC_PAGE_PATHS.jonangDoctrine,
    title: "The Jonang Tradition",
    body:
      "Go here for the best high-level overview of history, Zhentong philosophy, suppression, survival, and revival.",
  },
  {
    href: STATIC_PAGE_PATHS.kalachakra,
    title: "Kalachakra Practice",
    body:
      "Read this when you want to understand why Jonang is so closely linked with Kalachakra and completion-stage practice.",
  },
  {
    href: STATIC_PAGE_PATHS.curriculum,
    title: "Curriculum",
    body:
      "Use this page to see how study, ritual, retreat, and modern subjects fit together in one monastic path.",
  },
];
---

<div class="page-stack">
  <section class="editorial-intro">
    <div>
      <span class="editorial-kicker">Begin Here</span>
      <h2>A first orientation to the Jonang world</h2>
      <p class="editorial-lede">
        The Jonang tradition can feel dense at first because it brings together
        difficult philosophy, advanced tantric practice, and a complicated
        political history. The simplest way in is to treat it as a living
        tradition with three defining features.
      </p>
    </div>
    <aside class="editorial-panel">
      <span class="editorial-kicker">Three Things to Remember</span>
      <ul class="editorial-checklist">
        <li>Jonang is a complete Tibetan Buddhist school, not a minor appendix</li>
        <li>It is especially linked with Kalachakra and Zhentong</li>
        <li>Its modern life is shaped by both survival and resurgence</li>
      </ul>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="editorial-grid">
    <article class="editorial-card">
      <h3>History</h3>
      <p>
        Jonang emerges clearly in the thirteenth century, flourishes through
        masters like Dolpopa and Taranatha, and then survives a period of
        suppression by continuing in eastern Tibetan regions.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="editorial-card">
      <h3>View</h3>
      <p>
        The tradition is known for Zhentong, a way of speaking about ultimate
        reality as empty of obscurations yet radiant with awakened qualities.
      </p>
    </article>
    <article class="editorial-card">
      <h3>Practice</h3>
      <p>
        Jonang is deeply associated with the Kalachakra Tantra, especially its
        completion-stage yogas and retreat-based contemplative path.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="editorial-panel">
    <span class="editorial-kicker">How To Read This Site</span>
    <p>
      If you are new, begin with the broad pages first and only then move into
      the more technical material. The site is designed so that accessible
      summaries lead naturally into deeper sections rather than requiring you to
      master vocabulary at the start.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <span class="editorial-kicker">Recommended Path</span>
    <h2>Four strong first stops</h2>
    <div class="editorial-link-grid">
      {firstStops.map((item) => (
        <article class="editorial-link-card">
          <a href={item.href}>
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.body}</p>
          </a>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>
</div>
